    "https://jobs.utdallas.edu/postings/32742": {
      "title": "Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Assistant or Associate Professor) in Computational Neuroscience with a focus on Pain and/or Peripheral Neuropathy",
      "description": "University of Texas at Dallas Employment Opportunities | Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Assistant or Associate Professor) in Computational Neuroscience with a focus on Pain and/or Peripheral Neuropathy Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Assistant or Associate Professor) in Computational Neuroscience with a focus on Pain and/or Peripheral Neuropathy Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number F01181P Position Title Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Assistant or Associate Professor) in Computational Neuroscience with a focus on Pain and/or Peripheral Neuropathy Functional Title Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Assistant or Associate Professor) in Computational Neuroscience with a focus on Pain and/or Peripheral Neuropathy Department Neuroscience Salary Range Negotiable Pay Basis Monthly Position Status Academic Year Location Richardson Position End Date (if temporary) Posting Open Date 09/16/2026 Posting Close Date 03/31/2027 Open Until Filled No Desired Start Date 08/01/2027 Job Summary Position Description The Department of Neuroscience at The University of Texas at Dallas seeks to hire a tenure-track faculty member with expertise in transcriptomics and/or proteomics, neural computation, neural modeling, computational pharmacology, genomics, or using approaches at the intersection of these areas with a focus on research in pain and/or peripheral neuropathies. The successful candidate will be expected to integrate their research program with the unique strength in human molecular neuroscience and human peripheral nervous system physiology and pharmacology in the Center for Advanced Pain Studies ( CAPS ). The goal of this hiring initiative is to build upon our internationally recognized strength at the CAPS in pain neuroscience research and therapeutic discovery and development for pain and peripheral neuropathies. In addition to cutting-edge research in the pain and sensory neuroscience areas, work in CAPS at UT Dallas leverages and extends existing University expertise in systems and cognitive neuroscience, therapeutic development, human molecular neuroscience, neural device development, and artificial intelligence and machine learning. The ideal candidate would conduct highly collaborative dry lab research in areas of focus aligned with strengths in CAPS , the Department of Neuroscience, and the growing computational biology and neuroscience focus at UT Dallas. Faculty are expected to teach undergraduate and graduate courses related to their area of expertise. The appointment commences for the fall 2027 semester. Qualifications Minimum Education and Experience: A PhD in neuroscience, pharmacology, computer science, mathematics, or a related discipline is required prior to employment. Candidates are expected to demonstrate the ability to work effectively in a highly collaborative, engaging, and dynamic environment comprised of individuals with a range of backgrounds, skills, and perspectives. We are seeking candidates able to produce research and scholarly or creative achievements that enhance the program and the discipline, and able to deliver high quality teaching using evidence-based practices to effectively engage students from a range of backgrounds and experiences. Preferred Education and Experience: In addition to a PhD degree, competitive candidates would have postdoctoral training, robust publication records, and evidence of a dynamic and productive research program with clear potential for funding. UT Dallas School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences and the Department of Neuroscience The University of Texas at Dallas is a Carnegie R1 Research Institution and is among the fastest growing universities in the nation. The School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences ( BBS ) is one of seven schools at UT Dallas and ranked 2nd in total new research grants and research expenditures over the past year. BBS stands uniquely positioned to advance knowledge and improve lives through the efforts of its 119 faculty members and 3,200 students. In North Texas, BBS serves as a trailblazer where research, education, and clinical training come together to prepare future leaders. It is home to world-class faculty with research programs spanning bench-top to bedside, and ample resources and opportunities for students and faculty members alike. In the past year, BBS was recognized by the BlueRidge Institute for being #1 in Texas and #2 in the United States for NIH funding for schools of its type. Within BBS and the highly collaborative UT Dallas community, faculty members are surrounded by colleagues who interface with science and technology at every level of translation – with a keen focus on using our science to impact the health and well-being of people around the world while training the scientific and clinical leaders of tomorrow. Collectively, BBS leads UT Dallas in R&D rate and new awards per faculty as well as many student success metrics across the university. The Department of Neuroscience is one of three departments within BBS . It houses a dynamic and collaborative Neuroscience research community with extensive extramural funding and robust output of annual publications. The department houses 35 faculty members and approximately 1,200 students. The Department of Neuroscience is home to the BS in Neuroscience degree, representing the 5th largest and one of the most sought-after undergraduate degree programs on the UT Dallas campus. It is also home to the Systems and Cellular Neuroscience PhD program track, and shares degree programs with the Department of Psychology for the MS in Applied Cognition and Neuroscience and the PhD in Cognition and Neuroscience. The Department is housed in a cutting-edge research building that contains state-of-the-art laboratory space and top-notch core facilities. Additional opportunities for vibrant interdisciplinary collaborations are available with UT Dallas Centers including the Center for Vital Longevity, the Center for Children and Families, the Texas Biomedical Device Center, the Center for Advanced Pain Studies, and the Psychological Services and Education Center. BBS faculty also have vigorous collaborations with the UT Dallas Bioengineering and Electrical Engineering programs, the UT Southwestern Medical Center, and Baylor University Medical Center. The UT Dallas Clinical and Translational Research Center, led and directed under BBS , provides comprehensive support for clinical research including regulatory support, administrative staffing, and access to core facilities. Clinical and translational research programs are additionally facilitated by the UT Dallas Sammons BrainHealth Imaging Center. Application Instructions Applicants should upload the following: ·         Full curriculum vitae and cover letter summarizing their interests and qualifications for the position. ·         Statement of teaching philosophy describing their conceptualizations of teaching and learning, and teaching and assessment methods, and how their teaching practices will engage students from a range of backgrounds and experiences. ·         Research statement describing past, present, and future research, including how they mentor (or will mentor) student researchers and foster (or will foster) collaborative research environments. ·         A writing/publication sample; and ·         Full contact information for at least three academic or professional references. Priority will be given to completed applications received by December 31, 2026. Reviews will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed on March 31, 2027. The University and Community UT Dallas is a top public research university located in one of the nation’s fastest-growing metropolitan regions. Our seven schools offer 160 undergraduate and graduate degree programs, plus undergraduate and graduate certificates. Our student body is around 30,000 strong, reflecting students from over 100 countries and a multiplicity of perspectives and experiences. Over 60% of our undergraduate students receive some form of need- or merit-based financial aid, and 62% of graduating seniors have no student debt compared to 49% in all Texas publics and 51% in all national publics ( UT Dallas Fast Facts ). Under the dynamic leadership of President Prabhas V. Moghe, UT Dallas is charting a path built on three strategic pillars – student success, academic and research convergence, and external impact. Guided by the Roadmap 2030 plan, UT Dallas will accelerate its upward trajectory to become a university of even greater impact and distinction. UT Dallas is committed to graduating well-rounded members of the community whose education has prepared them for rewarding lives and productive careers in a constantly changing world. Our mission centers on providing Texas and the nation with excellent and innovative education and research. The University’s rapid growth is fueled by our creative and enterprising spirit, bright students, innovative programs, renowned faculty, dedicated staff, engaged alumni, and research that matters. The University promotes a welcoming environment through programs and initiatives designed to support engagement and success for members of the campus community. Employee benefits include a range of physical and mental wellness resources, competitive insurance and retirement plan options, lactation facilities located throughout the campus, and Employee Resource Groups (ERGs) comprised of individuals who share common interests to help build community among UT Dallas faculty and staff (e.g., Universal Access ERG , Military and Veteran ERG , UT Dallas Young Professionals). Additionally, the University’s modern campus, 400+ campus organizations, and prime location foster collaboration and community. Situated in Richardson, Texas, the University’s location offers abundant professional development and entertainment options. The Dallas-Fort Worth ( DFW ) metroplex is rich with visual and performing arts venues, museum districts, professional and semi-professional athletics teams, botanical gardens, accessible trails, and much more, ensuring there’s something for everyone. The University’s partnerships with regional higher education institutions, local school districts, numerous companies, and the Richardson Innovation Quarter (Richardson IQ) – a major hub for innovation, entrepreneurship, and educational activities – promote collaboration, professional growth, and educational excellence. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. The University prohibits unlawful discrimination against a person because of their race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, or veteran status. Minimum Education and Experience An earned graduate degree appropriate to the academic discipline and a record of productivity and professional achievement. Preferred Education and Experience In addition to a PhD degree, competitive candidates would have postdoctoral training, robust publication records, and evidence of a dynamic and productive research program with clear potential for funding. Other Qualifications To the extent this position requires the holder to research, work on, or have access to critical infrastructure as defined in Section 117.001(2) of the Texas Business and Commerce Code, the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the critical infrastructure is a minimum qualification to be hired and to continue to be employed in the position. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence Prepare and teach undergraduate and/or graduate classes Contribute assessment information and data as requested Mentor and/or advise undergraduate and/or graduate students Establish and/or continue an independent line of research Continue to expand professional influence in the academic discipline through research and/or publication Engage in service within the academic unit, the university, and the profession as appropriate based on teaching and research constraints Teach three (3) classes each academic year Physical Activities Working Conditions Additional Information Special Instructions Summary Important Message 1) All employees serve as a representative of the University and are expected to display respect, civility, professional courtesy, consideration of others and discretion in all interactions with members of the UT Dallas community and the general public. 2) The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living, and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. UT Dallas does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, age, national origin, disability, genetic information, or veteran status in its services, programs, activities, employment, and education, including in admission and enrollment. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request reasonable accommodation in the employment application and interview process, contact the ADA Coordinator . For inquiries regarding nondiscrimination policies, contact the Title IX Coordinator . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this employment opportunity? Governmental Agency Job Posting University's Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement in The Chronicle of Higher Education Advertisement in a Journal in my Academic Discipline Personal Referral Another Website Other * Which of the following most accurately describes your highest earned degree? Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) Doctor of Education (EdD) Other Doctoral Degree (DSci, DAud, MD, etc.) Specialist's Degree (EdS) Professional Degree Juris Doctor Master's Degree Bachelor's Degree Associate's Degree Certificate * In which Academic Discipline is your highest earned degree? (Open Ended Question) * How many years of relevant work experience do you have in Higher Education, Industry, or Research? None One year 2-3 years 4-5 years 6-10 years Over 10 years * In what areas do you hold any additional licenses or certifications valid in the State of Texas? (Open Ended Question) If your Ph.D. has not yet been awarded, when will your degree be awarded? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Teaching Philosophy Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Research Statement Optional Documents Unofficial Transcripts Other Document Relevant URL Veteran Employment Preference - Form DD-214 Teaching Evaluations Other Document Human Resources, 800 West Campbell Road, AD3.418 Richardson, TX 75080-3021 972.883.2221 Check Us Out on Social Media To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.utdallas.edu/postings/32746": {
      "title": "University of Texas",
      "description": "University of Texas at Dallas Employment Opportunities | CTRC Clinical Research Nurse Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help CTRC Clinical Research Nurse Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number S07236P Position Title CTRC Clinical Research Nurse Functional Title CTRC Clinical Research Nurse Department Neuroscience Salary Range $50,000 per year at 0.5 FTE (20 hours per week) Pay Basis Monthly Position Status Regular part-time Location Richardson Position End Date (if temporary) Posting Open Date 09/16/2026 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Desired Start Date 10/05/2026 Job Summary Join the University of Texas at Dallas Clinical and Translational Research Center ( CTRC ) and play a vital role in advancing innovative clinical research that impacts patient care and scientific discovery through a flexible part-time opportunity. The CTRC is seeking a Clinical Research Nurse who is passionate about research, participant safety, and delivering high-quality clinical care. The primary responsibility of this position is to support the safe, accurate, and protocol-compliant conduct of human-subjects research. The Research Nurse will perform protocol-directed research procedures and assessments involving research participants, including clinical measurements, specimen collection and processing, and participant education. The Research Nurse will promote research participant safety, recognize and appropriately escalate clinically significant findings or concerns arising during research activities, and ensure accurate documentation and adherence to study protocols and institutional requirements. The Research Nurse may actively participate in research participant recruitment and education and works collaboratively with the Medical Director, principal investigators, physicians, research coordinators and administrators, laboratory personnel, and other members of the research team. Minimum Education and Experience Education : Graduate of an accredited nursing program. Experience : At least three (3) years of nursing experience involving direct interaction with individuals in a clinical or research setting. Licenses and Certifications : Registered Nurse (RN) Current, unrestricted license to practice as a Registered Nurse in the State of Texas issued by the Texas Board of Nursing. Licensure must be current upon hire and maintained throughout employment. Basic Life Support ( BLS ) Current Basic Life Support certification through the American Heart Association ( AHA ) or American Red Cross ( ARC ). Certification must be current upon hire and maintained throughout employment. Preferred Education and Experience Preferred Education and Experience At least one (1) year of nursing experience in a human-subjects research setting involving direct interaction with research participants and performance of protocol-directed research procedures. Other Qualifications To the extent this position requires the holder to research, work on, or have access to critical infrastructure as defined in Section 117.001(2) of the Texas Business and Commerce Code, the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the critical infrastructure is a minimum qualification to be hired and to continue to be employed in the position. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Essential Duties and Responsibilities Perform protocol-directed research procedures and assessments in accordance with approved research protocols, study-specific procedures, institutional policies, and applicable research requirements. Perform phlebotomy and other protocol-required biological specimen collection. Process research specimens according to protocol and laboratory requirements, including centrifugation, aliquoting, labeling, handling, storage, and preparation for distribution or shipment. Maintain specimen integrity by adhering to protocol-specific processing timelines, temperature and storage requirements, laboratory procedures, and applicable biosafety and shipping requirements. Perform and document protocol-required clinical measurements and research assessments, which may include vital signs, ECG acquisition, and other study-specific procedures within the scope of RN practice and demonstrated competency. Monitor research participants during protocol-directed procedures; recognize abnormal findings, symptoms, adverse events, or other safety concerns; and promptly notify the appropriate investigator, Medical Director, or other designated research personnel in accordance with study and CTRC procedures. Initiate appropriate emergency response procedures when indicated. Maintain accurate, complete, and timely research documentation, including documentation of research procedures, assessments, specimen collection and processing, and other protocol-required activities. Identify and promptly communicate protocol deviations, specimen-processing errors, safety concerns, or other research-related issues to the appropriate investigator or CTRC leadership. Participate in research participant recruitment, screening, education, and informed-consent activities as permitted by the study protocol, IRB approval, institutional policy, and delegated study responsibilities. Review and follow study protocols, manuals of procedures, laboratory manuals, source documentation requirements, and other study-specific instructions necessary to perform assigned research activities. Complete and maintain required institutional and study-specific training, including human-subject research and Good Clinical Practice training and, when applicable to assigned responsibilities, training required for the handling, packaging, or shipment of biological specimens. Demonstrate competency in the safe operation of clinical, laboratory, and research equipment required for assigned protocols. Communicate effectively and professionally with research participants and members of multidisciplinary research teams. Work both independently and collaboratively while managing multiple research activities and maintaining attention to participant safety, protocol adherence, specimen integrity, and accurate documentation. Physical Demands and Working Conditions Physical Activities Working Conditions Additional Information Work Notice This is a part-time position scheduled for 20 hours per week. Work will be performed on-site and in person. What We Can Offer UT Dallas is an Equal Opportunity Employer with an employee-friendly and supportive work environment. Our comprehensive compensation and benefits package that is effective as of your hire date includes: Competitive Salary Tuition Benefits BCBS PPO Medical Insurance Options – including 100% paid employee medical coverage for full-time employees AD&D Insurance Dental & Vision Plan Options Long and short-term disability TRS Retirement Plan Fertility Benefits Paid time off All UT Dallas employees have access to various professional development opportunities, including a membership to Academic Impressions, LinkedIn Learning, and UT Dallas Bright Leaders Program. Visit https://hr.utdallas.edu/employees/benefits/ for more information. Special Instructions Summary Important Message 1) All employees serve as a representative of the University and are expected to display respect, civility, professional courtesy, consideration of others and discretion in all interactions with members of the UT Dallas community and the general public. 2) The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living, and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. UT Dallas does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, age, national origin, disability, genetic information, or veteran status in its services, programs, activities, employment, and education, including in admission and enrollment. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request reasonable accommodation in the employment application and interview process, contact the ADA Coordinator . For inquiries regarding nondiscrimination policies, contact the Title IX Coordinator . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application Optional Documents Veteran Employment Preference - Form DD-214 Human Resources, 800 West Campbell Road, AD3.418 Richardson, TX 75080-3021 972.883.2221 Check Us Out on Social Media To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.utdallas.edu/postings/32732": {
      "title": "Senior Director of AccessAbility Resource Center",
      "description": "University of Texas at Dallas Employment Opportunities | Senior Director of AccessAbility Resource Center Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Senior Director of AccessAbility Resource Center Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number S07238P Position Title Senior Director of AccessAbility Resource Center Functional Title Senior Director of AccessAbility Resource Center Department AccessAbility Resource Center Salary Range $120,000, Depending on Qualifications Pay Basis Monthly Position Status Regular full-time Location Richardson Position End Date (if temporary) Posting Open Date 09/11/2026 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Desired Start Date 01/01/2027 Job Summary Job Summary The Senior Director of the AccessAbility Resources Center ( ARC ) is a strategic leadership role responsible for overseeing university-wide accessibility services for students and ensuring compliance with federal and state regulations. Reporting to the Vice President for Academic Success & Innovation, this position serves as the University’s primary subject matter expert on ADA compliance, leading a dedicated team to provide comprehensive accommodations that enable students with disabilities to meet their academic potential. The Senior Director balances operational efficiency with long-term planning, focusing on removing barriers to education, modernizing accommodation workflows, and partnering with the Office of Information Technology, Educational Technology Services, and the Center for Teaching and Learning to advance digital accessibility standards. In addition to managing operations, the Senior Director works closely with faculty and academic leadership to provide professional development and advocate for student needs. The ideal candidate will demonstrate exceptional collaboration skills and advanced communication acumen, enabling them to build trust across a wide spectrum of stakeholders, including students, faculty, staff, and senior leadership, while navigating complex and sensitive conversations with empathy and clarity. The candidate will skillfully cultivate collaborative partnerships, promote a campus-wide culture grounded in respect, shared accountability, and drive strategic initiatives that meaningfully strengthen student engagement, access, and belonging. Minimum Education and Experience Bachelor’s degree with ten years of work experience in related field. Preferred Education and Experience Preferred Qualifications Master’s degree. Fourteen or more years of progressively responsible experience in a related field. Prior experience supervising and leading a complex team. Five years or more of experience in higher education. Other Qualifications To the extent this position requires the holder to research, work on, or have access to critical infrastructure as defined in Section 117.001(2) of the Texas Business and Commerce Code, the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the critical infrastructure is a minimum qualification to be hired and to continue to be employed in the position. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Key Responsibilities Lead the creation and execution of a strategic vision for the ARC , aligning accessibility goals directly with major institutional initiatives for student success. Use assessment data to evaluate the impact of services on student success and graduation rates, ensuring continuous improvement and responsiveness to student needs, while identifying campus-wide bottlenecks and forecasting future resource requirements. Serve as the primary subject matter expert on federal and state regulations ( ADA , Section 504/508, Fair Housing Act). Partner with Legal Counsel and Risk Management to interpret legislation, mitigate institutional liability, and guide leadership on best practices. Direct the daily operations of the ARC , including the intake process, exam administration, and assistive technology. Oversee the departmental budget and personnel, ensuring scalable workflows to handle increasing caseloads. Collaborate with academic leadership to transition the university from a retroactive accommodation model to a proactive Universal Design for Learning ( UDL ) framework. Develop and deliver training for faculty on accessible course design and navigating complex accommodation scenarios while maintaining academic integrity and respecting academic freedom. Partner with the Office of Information Technology ( OIT ) and Educational Technology Services to ensure digital assets meet WCAG 2.1 AA standards. Consult with Facilities Management on capital projects to ensure physical environments exceed minimum code requirements. Lead, mentor, and evaluate a diverse team of accessibility professionals. Foster a high-performance culture grounded in empathy, professional growth, and operational excellence. Work closely with students to ensure their feedback informs the development of ARC services, ensuring a consistent and inclusive experience across all aspects of university life, while actively fostering their self-advocacy skills and managing expectations around reasonable accommodations. Mediate complex situations between students and faculty, utilizing diplomatic persuasion to build buy-in for accessibility as a shared campus responsibility rather than solely a legal mandate. Physical Demands and Working Conditions Physical Activities Working Conditions Additional Information Required Skills & Competencies Demonstrated understanding of best practices for supporting student success, retention, and access in a research university setting. Proven ability to mentor and develop professional staff, encouraging growth and fostering a collaborative, high-performance team culture. Strong interpersonal and organizational skills, with the ability to engage with a wide range of stakeholders—including students, faculty, and executive leadership—to foster an environment of respect. Deep understanding of recent and emerging laws governing accessibility in higher education ( ADA , Section 504/508). Ability to exercise sound judgment, tact, discretion, and integrity when handling confidential information and sensitive conversations, backed by strong conflict resolution and de-escalation skills. Connection to national initiatives and trends related to accessibility, inclusive educational technologies, and Universal Design. Experience relating to budget oversight and resource allocation. Ability to act as a change agent, anticipating demographic and technological shifts, and leading without direct authority to drive proactive practices across academic micro-cultures. What We Can Offer UT Dallas is an Equal Opportunity Employer with an employee-friendly and supportive work environment. Our comprehensive compensation and benefits package that is effective as of your hire date includes: Competitive Salary Tuition Benefits Internal Training BCBS PPO Medical insurance – 100% paid for full-time employees Vision Insurance Long and short-term disability TRS Retirement Plan Dental/Vision/AD&D Paid Time off Fertility Benefits About Us UT Dallas is a top public research university located in one of the nation’s fastest-growing metropolitan regions. Our seven schools offer more than 140 undergraduate and graduate programs, plus professional certificates and fast-track programs. Our student body is 31,000 strong, reflecting students from over 100 countries and a multiplicity of identities and experiences. UT Dallas is committed to graduating well-rounded members of the global community whose education has prepared them for rewarding lives and productive careers in a constantly changing world. All UT Dallas employees have access to various professional development opportunities, including a membership to Academic Impressions, LinkedIn Learning, and UT Dallas Bright Leaders Program. Visit https://hr.utdallas.edu/employees/benefits/ for more information. Special Instructions Summary Important Message 1) All employees serve as a representative of the University and are expected to display respect, civility, professional courtesy, consideration of others and discretion in all interactions with members of the UT Dallas community and the general public. 2) The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living, and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. UT Dallas does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, age, national origin, disability, genetic information, or veteran status in its services, programs, activities, employment, and education, including in admission and enrollment. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request reasonable accommodation in the employment application and interview process, contact the ADA Coordinator . For inquiries regarding nondiscrimination policies, contact the Title IX Coordinator . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application Official Transcripts Optional Documents Veteran Employment Preference - Form DD-214 Human Resources, 800 West Campbell Road, AD3.418 Richardson, TX 75080-3021 972.883.2221 Check Us Out on Social Media To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.utdallas.edu/postings/32741": {
      "title": "Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) and Doctor of Audiology Program Head",
      "description": "University of Texas at Dallas Employment Opportunities | Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) and Doctor of Audiology Program Head Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) and Doctor of Audiology Program Head Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number F01179P Position Title Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) and Doctor of Audiology Program Head Functional Title Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) and Doctor of Audiology Program Head Department Speech Language Hearing Salary Range Negotiable Pay Basis Monthly Position Status Academic Year Location Dallas Position End Date (if temporary) Posting Open Date 09/16/2026 Posting Close Date 02/28/2027 Open Until Filled No Desired Start Date 08/01/2027 Job Summary Position Description The School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences ( BBS ) at The University of Texas at Dallas (UT Dallas) invites applications from outstanding clinician scientists to lead the highly ranked Doctor of Audiology (AuD) Program, housed in the Department of Speech, Language, and Hearing ( SLH ). Applicants must be eligible for licensure as an Audiologist in the state of Texas and have an established record of scholarship and leadership. The AuD program head is the most visible member of the program both within and outside the university. As such, they are expected to articulate a clear vision for the AuD program and prepare students for the changing landscape of healthcare. The AuD program has strong ties to the world-renowned Callier Center for Communication Disorders. The strong historic partnership with the Callier Center provides practicum placements, student scholarships, and support for shared education and research missions. Program Head responsibilities include overseeing the AuD curriculum and training program, teaching graduate and undergraduate courses, and leading AuD program administration, accreditation, and review processes. In addition to clinical credentials, applicants must hold a Ph.D. in Speech, Language, and Hearing Science or a related field (Communication Sciences, Cognitive Science, Neuroscience, Psychology), provide evidence of a dynamic and productive research program with extramural funding, and demonstrate a commitment to excellence in teaching. Applicant research programs may be in any area of hearing, balance, or associated disorders. Appointment will be at the rank of Associate or full Professor according to experience. The appointment commences for the fall 2027 semester. Qualifications Minimum Education and Experience: Eligibility for licensure as an Audiologist in the State of Texas is required. A PhD or equivalent in Speech, Language, and Hearing Science or in a related discipline (Communication Science, Cognitive Science, Neuroscience, Psychology) is required prior to employment. Candidates are expected to demonstrate the ability to work effectively in a highly collaborative, engaging, and dynamic environment comprised of individuals with a range of backgrounds, skills, and perspectives. We are seeking candidates able to produce research and scholarly or creative achievements that enhance the program and the discipline, and able to deliver high-quality teaching using evidence-based practices to effectively engage students from a range of backgrounds and experiences. Preferred Education and Experience: Candidates with a history of scholarship pertinent to hearing and hearing disorders, a strong publication portfolio, and record of extramural funding appropriate for their career stage are preferred. Candidates with a history of academic or other professional leadership are preferred. Candidates holding clinical certification by the American Board of Audiology ( ABA ) and/or the American Speech-Language-Hearing Association ( ASHA ) are preferred. UT Dallas School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences and the Department of Speech, Language, and Hearing The University of Texas at Dallas is a Carnegie R1 Research Institution and is among the fastest growing universities in the nation. The School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences ( BBS ) is one of seven schools at UT Dallas and ranked 2nd in total new research grants and research expenditures over the past year. BBS stands uniquely positioned to advance knowledge and improve lives through the efforts of its 119 faculty and 3,200 students. In North Texas, BBS serves as a trailblazer where research, education, and clinical training come together to prepare future leaders. It is home to world-class faculty with research programs spanning bench-top to bedside, and ample resources and opportunities for students and faculty alike. In the past year, BBS was recognized by the BlueRidge Institute for being #1 in Texas and #2 in the United States for NIH funding for schools of its type. Within BBS and the highly collaborative UT Dallas community, faculty members are surrounded by colleagues who interface with science and technology at every level of translation – with a keen focus on using our science to impact the health and well-being of people around the world while training the scientific and clinical leaders of tomorrow. Collectively, BBS leads UT Dallas in R&D rate and new awards per faculty as well as many student success metrics across the university. The Department of Speech, Language, and Hearing is one of three departments within BBS . It is home to a dynamic, collaborative research community that houses the nation’s #1 public Doctor of Audiology program (tied) and #10 public MS in Speech-Language Pathology program (tied) (based on 2026 U.S. News & World Report rankings). Faculty members actively engage in research and clinical practice at the Callier Center for Communication Disorders, advancing patient care and research at its two Dallas-Fort Worth locations. The department includes 35 faculty members and approximately 450 students. The department offers the BS in Speech, Language, and Hearing Sciences, the MS in Speech-Language Pathology, the AuD in Audiology, and the PhD in Speech, Language, and Hearing Sciences. Additional opportunities for vibrant interdisciplinary collaborations are available with UT Dallas Centers including the Center for Vital Longevity, the Center for Children and Families, the Texas Biomedical Device Center, the Center for Advanced Pain Studies, and the Psychological Services and Education Center. BBS faculty also have vigorous collaborations with the UT Dallas Bioengineering and Electrical Engineering programs, the UT Southwestern Medical Center, and Baylor University Medical Center. The UT Dallas Clinical and Translational Research Center, led and directed under BBS , provides comprehensive support for clinical research including regulatory support, administrative staffing, and access to core facilities. Clinical and translational research programs are additionally facilitated by the UT Dallas Sammons BrainHealth Imaging Center. Application Instructions Applicants should upload the following via the online application : · Full curriculum vitae and cover letter summarizing their interests and qualifications for the position. · Statement of teaching philosophy describing their conceptualizations of teaching and learning, and teaching and assessment methods, and how their teaching practices will engage students from a range of backgrounds and experiences. · Research statement describing past, present, and future research, including how they mentor (or will mentor) student researchers and foster (or will foster) collaborative research environments. · A writing/publication sample. · Full contact information for at least three academic or professional references. Priority will be given to completed applications received by November 1, 2026. Reviews will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed on February 28, 2027. The University and Community UT Dallas is a top public research university located in one of the nation’s fastest-growing metropolitan regions. Our seven schools offer 160 undergraduate and graduate degree programs, plus undergraduate and graduate certificates. Our student body is around 30,000 strong, reflecting students from over 100 countries and a multiplicity of perspectives and experiences. Over 60% of our undergraduate students receive some form of need- or merit-based financial aid, and 62% of graduating seniors have no student debt compared to 49% in all Texas publics and 51% in all national publics ( UT Dallas Fast Facts ). Under the dynamic leadership of President Prabhas V. Moghe, UT Dallas is charting a path built on three strategic pillars – student success, academic and research convergence, and external impact. Guided by the Roadmap 2030 plan, UT Dallas will accelerate its upward trajectory to become a university of even greater impact and distinction. UT Dallas is committed to graduating well-rounded members of the community whose education has prepared them for rewarding lives and productive careers in a constantly changing world. Our mission centers on providing Texas and the nation with excellent and innovative education and research. The University’s rapid growth is fueled by our creative and enterprising spirit, bright students, innovative programs, renowned faculty, dedicated staff, engaged alumni, and research that matters. The University promotes a welcoming environment through programs and initiatives designed to support engagement and success for members of the campus community. Employee benefits include a range of physical and mental wellness resources, competitive insurance and retirement plan options, lactation facilities located throughout the campus, and Employee Resource Groups (ERGs) comprised of individuals who share common interests to help build community among UT Dallas faculty and staff (e.g., Universal Access ERG , Military and Veteran ERG , UT Dallas Young Professionals). Additionally, the University’s modern campus, 400+ campus organizations, and prime location foster collaboration and community. Situated in Richardson, Texas, the University’s location offers abundant professional development and entertainment options. The Dallas-Fort Worth ( DFW ) metroplex is rich with visual and performing arts venues, museum districts, professional and semi-professional athletics teams, botanical gardens, accessible trails, and much more, ensuring there’s something for everyone. The University’s partnerships with regional higher education institutions, local school districts, numerous companies, and the Richardson Innovation Quarter (Richardson IQ) – a major hub for innovation, entrepreneurship, and educational activities – promote collaboration, professional growth, and educational excellence. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. The University prohibits unlawful discrimination against a person because of their race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, or veteran status. Minimum Education and Experience An earned graduate degree appropriate to the academic discipline and a record of productivity and professional achievement. Preferred Education and Experience Candidates with a history of scholarship pertinent to hearing and hearing disorders, a strong publication portfolio, and record of extramural funding appropriate for their career stage are preferred. Candidates with a history of academic or other professional leadership are preferred. Candidates holding clinical certification by the American Board of Audiology ( ABA ) and/or the American Speech-Language-Hearing Association ( ASHA ) are preferred. Other Qualifications To the extent this position requires the holder to research, work on, or have access to critical infrastructure as defined in Section 117.001(2) of the Texas Business and Commerce Code, the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the critical infrastructure is a minimum qualification to be hired and to continue to be employed in the position. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence Prepare and teach undergraduate and/or graduate classes Contribute assessment information and data as requested Mentor and/or advise undergraduate and/or graduate students Establish and/or continue an independent line of research Continue to expand professional influence in the academic discipline through research and/or publication Engage in service within the academic unit, the university, and the profession as appropriate based on teaching and research constraints Teach two (2) classes each academic year Provide leadership as Head of the Doctor of Audiology Program Physical Activities Working Conditions Additional Information A supplemental document be required, responding to the following prompt: AuD Education Vision What characteristics distinguish a truly outstanding AuD program from other programs in the field? Please describe the elements you believe are essential to achieving excellence in AuD education and training, and how you would envision advancing our program toward those standards as Program Head. Special Instructions Summary Important Message 1) All employees serve as a representative of the University and are expected to display respect, civility, professional courtesy, consideration of others and discretion in all interactions with members of the UT Dallas community and the general public. 2) The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living, and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. UT Dallas does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, age, national origin, disability, genetic information, or veteran status in its services, programs, activities, employment, and education, including in admission and enrollment. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request reasonable accommodation in the employment application and interview process, contact the ADA Coordinator . For inquiries regarding nondiscrimination policies, contact the Title IX Coordinator . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this employment opportunity? Governmental Agency Job Posting University's Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement in The Chronicle of Higher Education Advertisement in a Journal in my Academic Discipline Personal Referral Another Website Other * Which of the following most accurately describes your highest earned degree? Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) Doctor of Education (EdD) Other Doctoral Degree (DSci, DAud, MD, etc.) Specialist's Degree (EdS) Professional Degree Juris Doctor Master's Degree Bachelor's Degree Associate's Degree Certificate * In which Academic Discipline is your highest earned degree? (Open Ended Question) * How many years of relevant work experience do you have in Higher Education, Industry, or Research? None One year 2-3 years 4-5 years 6-10 years Over 10 years * In what areas do you hold any additional licenses or certifications valid in the State of Texas? (Open Ended Question) If your Ph.D. has not yet been awarded, when will your degree be awarded? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Teaching Philosophy Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Other Document Teaching Evaluations Research Statement Optional Documents Unofficial Transcripts Relevant URL Veteran Employment Preference - Form DD-214 Other Document Human Resources, 800 West Campbell Road, AD3.418 Richardson, TX 75080-3021 972.883.2221 Check Us Out on Social Media To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
