    "https://jobs.utdallas.edu/postings/32747": {
      "title": "Research Scientist",
      "description": "University of Texas at Dallas Employment Opportunities | Research Scientist Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Research Scientist Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number S07222P Position Title Research Scientist Functional Title Department Space Sciences Salary Range $65,000-$75,000 Dependent on Qualifications Pay Basis Monthly Position Status Regular full-time Location Richardson Position End Date (if temporary) Posting Open Date 09/16/2026 Posting Close Date Open Until Filled Yes Desired Start Date 11/02/2026 Job Summary The University of Texas at Dallas ( UTD ) is seeking to fill a Research Scientist in the W. B. Hanson Center for Space Sciences. The Center has a vigorous research program associated with fundamental and applied studies of the Earth’s ionosphere-thermosphere-magnetosphere ( ITM ) system using space and ground-based experimental resources as well as numerical modeling. There are currently nine Earth-orbiting platforms with CSS thermal plasma instruments on board. The Center has been selected to provide the Thermal Plasma Sensor ( TPS ) instrument for NASA’s Geospace Dynamics Constellation ( GDC ) Trailblazer mission with the expectation of future GDC missions. GDC is a satellite mission designed to investigate the coupling between the ionosphere and thermosphere and their response to external energy inputs. It will dramatically improve our understanding of the near-Earth environment and how disturbances (space weather) there impact human technology and society. The candidate will be expected to be a major part of this exciting mission. Minimum Education and Experience Master’s degree in a field directly related to specified research area and two (2) years of directly applicable experience conducting research related to the specified field of study. Preferred Education and Experience A PhD or equivalent in space physics or a closely related field and a demonstrated commitment to excellence in research. Other Qualifications To the extent this position requires the holder to research, work on, or have access to critical infrastructure as defined in Section 117.001(2) of the Texas Business and Commerce Code, the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the critical infrastructure is a minimum qualification to be hired and to continue to be employed in the position. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Candidate for this position should be capable of complementing the research capabilities of the Center, in particular in the area of ionosphere-thermosphere coupling. The candidate will be especially involved in the development of analysis tools and research associated with the thermal plasma instruments that are a major part of the Center’s historical and ongoing efforts. Physical Demands and Working Conditions Physical Activities Working Conditions Additional Information Candidate is expected to work effectively in a highly collaborative, engaging, and dynamic environment comprised of individuals of varied backgrounds, skills, and perspectives. A remote work schedule is not available for this position. Special Instructions Summary Important Message 1) All employees serve as a representative of the University and are expected to display respect, civility, professional courtesy, consideration of others and discretion in all interactions with members of the UT Dallas community and the general public. 2) The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living, and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. UT Dallas does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, age, national origin, disability, genetic information, or veteran status in its services, programs, activities, employment, and education, including in admission and enrollment. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request reasonable accommodation in the employment application and interview process, contact the ADA Coordinator . For inquiries regarding nondiscrimination policies, contact the Title IX Coordinator . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application Optional Documents Veteran Employment Preference - Form DD-214 Human Resources, 800 West Campbell Road, AD3.418 Richardson, TX 75080-3021 972.883.2221 Check Us Out on Social Media To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.utdallas.edu/postings/32739": {
      "title": "Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) in Psychology",
      "description": "University of Texas at Dallas Employment Opportunities | Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) in Psychology Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) in Psychology Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number F01180P Position Title Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) in Psychology Functional Title Open Rank Tenure Track Faculty Position (Associate or Full Professor) in Psychology Department Psychology Salary Range Negotiable Pay Basis Monthly Position Status Academic Year Location Richardson Position End Date (if temporary) Posting Open Date 09/16/2026 Posting Close Date 04/01/2027 Open Until Filled No Desired Start Date 08/01/2027 Job Summary Position Description The Department of Psychology in the School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences ( BBS ) at the University of Texas at Dallas (UT Dallas) is accepting applications for a tenure system faculty position in Psychology. The search is focused on finding an excellent candidate with research interests related to Brain Health. This candidate will also have leadership experience and/or expertise to serve as the inaugural head of the Brain Health BS program. BBS is actively working toward approval of this new Bachelor of Science in Brain Health that will be housed in the Department of Psychology but will cut across all departments in BBS and with strong partnerships across multiple schools on campus. This new degree program will serve as a gateway for creation of a new arm in our workforce with strong support from the DFW corporate and industry community. The Center for BrainHealth is a recognized world leader in the study of Brain Health and for 25 years has led the DFW community in creating recognition for the importance of brain health and states of disease in everyday life. Candidates at Associate or Full Professor levels with funded research programs will be considered. Teaching responsibilities will contribute to the department’s undergraduate, master’s, and doctoral programs in Psychology and the new BS in Brain Health. Teaching responsibilities also include mentoring of doctoral, masters, and undergraduate students. The appointment commences for the fall 2027 semester. Qualifications Minimum Education and Experience: A PhD or equivalent in Psychology, Cognition and Neuroscience, or a related discipline is required and a track record of obtaining and maintaining extramural research funding that is commensurate with appointment at the rank of Associate or Full Professor. Candidates are expected to demonstrate the ability to work effectively in a highly collaborative, engaging, and dynamic environment comprised of individuals with a range of backgrounds, skills, and perspectives. We are seeking candidates able to produce research and scholarly or creative achievements that enhance the program and the discipline, and able to deliver high-quality teaching using evidence-based practices to effectively engage students from a range of backgrounds and experiences. Preferred Education and Experience: demonstration of leadership experience. UT Dallas School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences and the Department of Psychology The University of Texas at Dallas is a Carnegie R1 Research Institution and is the fastest growing university in Texas. The School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences ( BBS ) is one of seven schools at UT Dallas and ranked 2nd in total new research grants and research expenditures over the past year. BBS stands uniquely positioned to advance knowledge and improve lives through the efforts of its 119 faculty members and 3,200 students. In North Texas, BBS serves as a trailblazer where research, education, and clinical training come together to prepare future leaders. It is home to world-class faculty with work ranging from bench-top to bedside studies, and ample resources and opportunities for students and faculty alike. In the past year, BBS was recognized by the BlueRidge Institute for being #1 in Texas and #2 in the United States for NIH funding for schools of its type. Within BBS and the highly collaborative UT Dallas community, faculty members are surrounded by colleagues who interface with science and technology at every level of translation – with a keen focus on using our science to impact the health and well-being of people around the world while training the scientific and clinical leaders of tomorrow. Collectively, BBS leads UT Dallas in R&D rate and new awards per faculty as well as many student success metrics across the university. The Department of Psychology is one of three departments within the School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences. The department houses 48 faculty and has almost 1,500 students. It is home to a large number of multi-grant funded faculty members. The department has a distinguished legacy of world class research across cognitive neuroscience and developmental science. It also houses multiple pioneering AI-engaged faculty innovating in the fields of cognitive science and human-computer interface research. The Department has a rich history of housing well-known research Centers that lead their respective fields around the globe. The Department of Psychology is home to the Bachelor of Science in Psychology, BS in Cognitive Science, and BS in Child Learning and Development. It is also home to the MS in Psychology, the MS in Human Development and Childhood Disorders, and the PhD in Psychology. It also shares degree programs with the Department of Neuroscience for the MS in Applied Cognition and Neuroscience and the PhD in Cognition and Neuroscience. The BS in Psychology represents the 7th most popular undergraduate major at UT Dallas. As of fall 2026, the department enrolled its first cohort of Clinical Psychological Science PhD students along with the opening of our Psychological Sciences and Education Center in summer 2026. Additional opportunities for vibrant interdisciplinary collaborations are available with UT Dallas Centers including the Center for Vital Longevity, the Center for Children and Families, the Texas Biomedical Device Center, the Center for Advanced Pain Studies, and the Psychological Services and Education Center. BBS faculty also have vigorous collaborations with the UT Dallas Bioengineering and Electrical Engineering programs, the UT Southwestern Medical Center, and Baylor University Medical Center. The UT Dallas Clinical and Translational Research Center, led and directed under BBS , provides comprehensive support for clinical research including regulatory support, administrative staffing, and access to core facilities. Clinical and translational research programs are additionally facilitated by the UT Dallas Sammons BrainHealth Imaging Center. Application Instructions Applicants should upload the following: ·         Full curriculum vitae and cover letter summarizing their interests and qualifications for the position. ·         Statement of teaching philosophy describing their conceptualizations of teaching and learning, and teaching and assessment methods, and how their teaching practices will engage students from a range of backgrounds and experiences. ·         Research statement describing past, present, and future research, including how they mentor (or will mentor) student researchers and foster (or will foster) collaborative research environments. ·         Full contact information for at least three academic or professional references. Priority will be given to completed applications received by November 15th. Reviews will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed on April 1, 2027. The University and Community UT Dallas is a top public research university located in one of the nation’s fastest-growing metropolitan regions. Our seven schools offer 160 undergraduate and graduate degree programs, plus undergraduate and graduate certificates. Our student body is around 30,000 strong, reflecting students from over 100 countries and a multiplicity of perspectives and experiences. Over 60% of our undergraduate students receive some form of need- or merit-based financial aid, and 62% of graduating seniors have no student debt compared to 49% in all Texas publics and 51% in all national publics ( UT Dallas Fast Facts ). Under the dynamic leadership of President Prabhas V. Moghe, UT Dallas is charting a path built on three strategic pillars – student success, academic and research convergence, and external impact. Guided by the Roadmap 2030 plan, UT Dallas will accelerate its upward trajectory to become a university of even greater impact and distinction. UT Dallas is committed to graduating well-rounded members of the community whose education has prepared them for rewarding lives and productive careers in a constantly changing world. Our mission centers on providing Texas and the nation with excellent and innovative education and research. The University’s rapid growth is fueled by our creative and enterprising spirit, bright students, innovative programs, renowned faculty, dedicated staff, engaged alumni, and research that matters. The University promotes a welcoming environment through programs and initiatives designed to support engagement and success for members of the campus community. Employee benefits include a range of physical and mental wellness resources, competitive insurance and retirement plan options, lactation facilities located throughout the campus, and Employee Resource Groups (ERGs) comprised of individuals who share common interests to help build community among UT Dallas faculty and staff (e.g., Universal Access ERG , Military and Veteran ERG , UT Dallas Young Professionals). Additionally, the University’s modern campus, 400+ campus organizations, and prime location foster collaboration and community. Situated in Richardson, Texas, the University’s location offers abundant professional development and entertainment options. The Dallas-Fort Worth ( DFW ) metroplex is rich with visual and performing arts venues, museum districts, professional and semi-professional athletics teams, botanical gardens, accessible trails, and much more, ensuring there’s something for everyone. The University’s partnerships with regional higher education institutions, local school districts, numerous companies, and the Richardson Innovation Quarter (Richardson IQ) – a major hub for innovation, entrepreneurship, and educational activities – promote collaboration, professional growth, and educational excellence. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. The University prohibits unlawful discrimination against a person because of their race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, or veteran status. Minimum Education and Experience An earned graduate degree appropriate to the academic discipline and a record of productivity and professional achievement. Preferred Education and Experience Demonstration of leadership service is preferred. Other Qualifications To the extent this position requires the holder to research, work on, or have access to critical infrastructure as defined in Section 117.001(2) of the Texas Business and Commerce Code, the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the critical infrastructure is a minimum qualification to be hired and to continue to be employed in the position. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence Prepare and teach undergraduate and/or graduate classes Contribute assessment information and data as requested Mentor and/or advise undergraduate and/or graduate students Establish and/or continue an independent line of research Continue to expand professional influence in the academic discipline through research and/or publication Engage in service within the academic unit, the university, and the profession as appropriate based on teaching and research constraints Teach one (1) to three (3) classes each academic year Physical Activities Working Conditions Additional Information Special Instructions Summary Important Message 1) All employees serve as a representative of the University and are expected to display respect, civility, professional courtesy, consideration of others and discretion in all interactions with members of the UT Dallas community and the general public. 2) The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living, and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. UT Dallas does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, age, national origin, disability, genetic information, or veteran status in its services, programs, activities, employment, and education, including in admission and enrollment. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request reasonable accommodation in the employment application and interview process, contact the ADA Coordinator . For inquiries regarding nondiscrimination policies, contact the Title IX Coordinator . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this employment opportunity? Governmental Agency Job Posting University's Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement in The Chronicle of Higher Education Advertisement in a Journal in my Academic Discipline Personal Referral Another Website Other * Which of the following most accurately describes your highest earned degree? Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) Doctor of Education (EdD) Other Doctoral Degree (DSci, DAud, MD, etc.) Specialist's Degree (EdS) Professional Degree Juris Doctor Master's Degree Bachelor's Degree Associate's Degree Certificate * In which Academic Discipline is your highest earned degree? (Open Ended Question) * How many years of relevant work experience do you have in Higher Education, Industry, or Research? None One year 2-3 years 4-5 years 6-10 years Over 10 years * In what areas do you hold any additional licenses or certifications valid in the State of Texas? (Open Ended Question) If your Ph.D. has not yet been awarded, when will your degree be awarded? (Open Ended Question) Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Teaching Philosophy Curriculum Vitae Teaching Evaluations Research Statement Optional Documents Unofficial Transcripts Writing Sample Other Document Relevant URL Veteran Employment Preference - Form DD-214 Other Document Human Resources, 800 West Campbell Road, AD3.418 Richardson, TX 75080-3021 972.883.2221 Check Us Out on Social Media To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.utdallas.edu/postings/32753": {
      "title": "Research Assistant I",
      "description": "University of Texas at Dallas Employment Opportunities | Research Assistant I Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Research Assistant I Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Please see Special Instructions for more details. Please direct questions about the position to Dr. Kendra Seaman ( Kendra.Seaman@utdallas.edu ). Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number S07245P Position Title Research Assistant I Functional Title Research Assistant I Department Center for Vital Longevity Salary Range Starting at $37,000.00 Dependent on Qualifications Pay Basis Monthly Position Status Regular full-time Location Dallas Position End Date (if temporary) Posting Open Date 09/16/2026 Posting Close Date 09/28/2026 Open Until Filled No Desired Start Date 10/01/2026 Job Summary The Aging Well Lab in the Center for Vital Longevity at The University of Texas at Dallas, under the direction of Kendra Seaman, Ph.D., is currently seeking to hire a full-time Research Assistant I beginning in October to assist with a newly funded research project. The position requires a minimum one-year commitment (renewable). We are interested in what motivates people, how that influences their decisions, and how this may change across adulthood. We use a wide variety of behavioral, modeling, and neuroimaging techniques. Please see https://agingwell.utdallas.edu/ for more information on our lab’s focus and ongoing projects. Minimum Education and Experience Bachelor’s degree in one of the basic sciences or an equivalent combination of education and experience in customer service or in daily interactions in a public services environment. Preferred Education and Experience Preferred qualifications include a BA or BS in psychology, neuroscience, behavioral economics, data science, or a related field. Strong applicants from other fields may be considered. Experience with human subjects data collection, excellent organizational, time management, problem-solving, interpersonal, and communication skills are required. Prior experience with experimental design, programming of behavioral tasks, and data analysis. Technical skills (e.g. Python, R, Unix) are also highly desired but not required. Prior experience managing teams and/or an interest in psychological and neurobiological changes across adulthood are also a plus. Other Qualifications To the extent this position requires the holder to research, work on, or have access to critical infrastructure as defined in Section 117.001(2) of the Texas Business and Commerce Code, the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the critical infrastructure is a minimum qualification to be hired and to continue to be employed in the position. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Primary responsibilities for the Research Assistant will include (1) assisting with the design and implementation of online and in-person behavioral experiments, (2) recruitment and testing of human subjects, (3) coordinating and training research assistants, and (4) preprocessing and analysis of experimental data. Physical Demands and Working Conditions Physical Activities Working Conditions Additional Information Remote work is not available for this position. Special Instructions Summary Please direct questions about the position to Dr. Kendra Seaman ( Kendra.Seaman@utdallas.edu ). Important Message 1) All employees serve as a representative of the University and are expected to display respect, civility, professional courtesy, consideration of others and discretion in all interactions with members of the UT Dallas community and the general public. 2) The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living, and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. UT Dallas does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, age, national origin, disability, genetic information, or veteran status in its services, programs, activities, employment, and education, including in admission and enrollment. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request reasonable accommodation in the employment application and interview process, contact the ADA Coordinator . For inquiries regarding nondiscrimination policies, contact the Title IX Coordinator . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). Applicant Documents Required Documents Resume Cover Letter/Letter of Application Optional Documents Curriculum Vitae Veteran Employment Preference - Form DD-214 Human Resources, 800 West Campbell Road, AD3.418 Richardson, TX 75080-3021 972.883.2221 Check Us Out on Social Media To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
    },
    "https://jobs.utdallas.edu/postings/32767": {
      "title": "Assistant Professor Tenure Track Faculty Position in Speech, Language, and Hearing",
      "description": "University of Texas at Dallas Employment Opportunities | Assistant Professor Tenure Track Faculty Position in Speech, Language, and Hearing Skip to Main Content Toggle navigation Home Search Jobs Job Alerts Log In /Create Account Help Assistant Professor Tenure Track Faculty Position in Speech, Language, and Hearing Bookmark this Posting Print Preview | Apply for this Job Posting Details Posting Details Posting Number F01184P Position Title Assistant Professor Tenure Track Faculty Position in Speech, Language, and Hearing Functional Title Assistant Professor Tenure Track Faculty Position in Speech, Language, and Hearing Department Speech Language Hearing Salary Range Negotiable Pay Basis Monthly Position Status Academic Year Location Dallas Position End Date (if temporary) Posting Open Date 09/17/2026 Posting Close Date 02/28/2027 Open Until Filled No Desired Start Date 08/01/2027 Job Summary Position Description The Department of Speech, Language, and Hearing at The University of Texas at Dallas (UT Dallas) seeks to fill a tenure-track, Assistant Professor position. Applications are invited from across the speech, language, and hearing disciplines. Successful applicants will have expertise in the use, development, or application of artificial intelligence (AI) driven tools to advance the understanding of speech, language, and/or hearing processes. Priority areas include the development and application of AI driven tools to study typical and atypical speech, language, and hearing processes and their development; early detection and identification of communication disorders across the lifespan; innovative new devices and technologies that improve the efficiency, accessibility, or effectiveness of patient care; and/or virtual reality programs that enhance clinical education and clinical learning outcomes. The position requires an active research program with publications in peer-reviewed journals, current or clear potential for extramural funding, and excellence in teaching and service within the UT Dallas and professional communities. Faculty members teach undergraduate and graduate level courses within their discipline and area of expertise. Cross-listing of undergraduate and graduate seminars facilitates multi-disciplinary student education. Clinical credentials are not required. The appointment commences for the fall 2027 semester. Qualifications Minimum Education and Experience: A PhD or equivalent in Speech, Language, and Hearing Science or a related discipline (Communication Science, Cognitive Science, Neuroscience, Psychology, Computer Science) is required prior to employment. Candidates are expected to demonstrate the ability to work effectively in a highly collaborative, engaging, and dynamic environment comprised of individuals with a range of backgrounds, skills, and perspectives. We are seeking candidates able to produce research and scholarly or creative achievements that enhance the program and the discipline, and able to deliver high quality teaching using evidence-based practices to effectively engage students from a range of backgrounds and experiences. Preferred Education and Experience: Postdoctoral experience is preferred. Candidates with a strong publication portfolio appropriate for their career stage are preferred; candidates with a history of and clear potential for future extramural funding are preferred. Successful applicants will have expertise in the use, development, or application of artificial intelligence (AI) driven tools in understanding typical and atypical speech, language, and hearing processes and development and/or innovations that enhance efficiency, accessibility, or effectiveness of patient care and related clinical education efforts. UT Dallas School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences and the Department of Speech, Language, and Hearing The University of Texas at Dallas is a Carnegie R1 Research Institution and is among the fastest growing universities in the nation. The School of Behavioral and Brain Sciences ( BBS ) is one of seven schools at UT Dallas and ranked 2nd in total new research grants and research expenditures over the past year. BBS stands uniquely positioned to advance knowledge and improve lives through the efforts of its 119 faculty members and 3,200 students. In North Texas, BBS serves as a trailblazer where research, education, and clinical training come together to prepare future leaders. It is home to world-class faculty with work ranging from bench-top to bedside studies, and ample resources and opportunities for students and faculty alike. In the past year, BBS was recognized by the BlueRidge Institute for being #1 in Texas and #2 in the United States for NIH funding for schools of its type. Within BBS , faculty members are surrounded by colleagues who interface with science and technology at every level of translation – with a keen focus on using our science to impact the health and well-being of people around the world while training the scientific leaders of tomorrow. Collectively, BBS leads UT Dallas in R&D rate and new awards per faculty member as well as many student success metrics across the university. The Department of Speech, Language, and Hearing is one of three departments within BBS . It is home to a dynamic, collaborative research community that houses the nation’s #1 public Doctor of Audiology program (tied) and #10 MS in Speech-Language Pathology program (tied), based on 2026 U.S. News & World Report rankings. Faculty members actively engage in research and clinical practice at the Callier Center for Communication Disorders, advancing patient care and research at its two Dallas-Fort Worth locations. The department includes 35 faculty members and approximately 450 students. The department offers the BS in Speech, Language, and Hearing Sciences, the MS in Speech-Language Pathology, the PhD in Speech, Language, and Hearing Sciences, and the AuD in Audiology. Additional opportunities for vibrant interdisciplinary collaborations are available with UT Dallas Centers including the Center for Vital Longevity, the Center for Children and Families, the Texas Biomedical Device Center, the Center for Advanced Pain Studies, and the Psychological Services and Education Center. BBS faculty also have vigorous collaborations with the UT Dallas Bioengineering and Electrical Engineering programs, the UT Southwestern Medical Center, and Baylor University Medical Center. The UT Dallas Clinical and Translational Research Center, led and directed under BBS , provides comprehensive support for clinical research including regulatory support, administrative staffing, and access to core facilities. Clinical and translational research programs are additionally facilitated by the UT Dallas Sammons BrainHealth Imaging Center. Application Instructions Applicants should upload the following: · Full curriculum vitae and cover letter summarizing their interests and qualifications for the position. · Statement of teaching philosophy describing their conceptualizations of teaching and learning, and teaching and assessment methods, and how their teaching practices will engage students from a range of backgrounds and experiences. · Research statement describing past, present, and future research, including how they mentor (or will mentor) student researchers and foster (or will foster) collaborative research environments. · A writing/publication sample. · Full contact information for at least three academic or professional references. Priority will be given to completed applications received by November 1, 2026. Reviews will continue until the position is filled or the search is closed on February 28, 2027. The University and Community UT Dallas is a top public research university located in one of the nation’s fastest-growing metropolitan regions. Our seven schools offer 160 undergraduate and graduate degree programs, plus undergraduate and graduate certificates. Our student body is around 30,000 strong, reflecting students from over 100 countries and a multiplicity of perspectives and experiences. Over 60% of our undergraduate students receive some form of need- or merit-based financial aid, and 62% of graduating seniors have no student debt compared to 49% in all Texas publics and 51% in all national publics ( UT Dallas Fast Facts ). Under the dynamic leadership of President Prabhas V. Moghe, UT Dallas is charting a path built on three strategic pillars – student success, academic and research convergence, and external impact. Guided by the Roadmap 2030 plan, UT Dallas will accelerate its upward trajectory to become a university of even greater impact and distinction. UT Dallas is committed to graduating well-rounded members of the community whose education has prepared them for rewarding lives and productive careers in a constantly changing world. Our mission centers on providing Texas and the nation with excellent and innovative education and research. The University’s rapid growth is fueled by our creative and enterprising spirt, bright students, innovative programs, renowned faculty, dedicated staff, engaged alumni, and research that matters. The University promotes a welcoming environment through programs and initiatives designed to support engagement and success for members of the campus community. Employee benefits include a range of physical and mental wellness resources, competitive insurance and retirement plan options, lactation facilities located throughout the campus, and Employee Resource Groups (ERGs) comprised of individuals who share common interests to help build community among UT Dallas faculty and staff (e.g., Universal Access ERG , Military and Veteran ERG , UT Dallas Young Professionals). Additionally, the University’s modern campus, 400+ campus organizations, and prime location foster collaboration and community. Situated in Richardson, Texas, the University’s location offers abundant professional development and entertainment options. The Dallas-Fort Worth ( DFW ) metroplex is rich with visual and performing arts venues, museum districts, professional and semi-professional athletics teams, botanical gardens, accessible trails, and much more, ensuring there’s something for everyone. The University’s partnerships with regional higher education institutions, local school districts, numerous companies, and the Richardson Innovation Quarter (Richardson IQ) – a major hub for innovation, entrepreneurship, and educational activities – promote collaboration, professional growth, and educational excellence. Equal Employment Opportunity The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. The University prohibits unlawful discrimination against a person because of their race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, national origin, age, disability, genetic information, or veteran status. Minimum Education and Experience An earned graduate degree appropriate to the academic discipline and a record of productivity and professional achievement. Preferred Education and Experience Postdoctoral experience is preferred. Candidates with a strong publication portfolio appropriate for their career stage are preferred; candidates with a history of and clear potential for future extramural funding are preferred. Successful applicants will have expertise in the use, development, or application of artificial intelligence (AI) driven tools in understanding typical and atypical speech, language, and hearing processes and development and/or innovations that enhance efficiency, accessibility, or effectiveness of patient care and related clinical education efforts. Other Qualifications To the extent this position requires the holder to research, work on, or have access to critical infrastructure as defined in Section 117.001(2) of the Texas Business and Commerce Code, the ability to maintain the security or integrity of the critical infrastructure is a minimum qualification to be hired and to continue to be employed in the position. Essential Duties and Responsibilities Demonstrate a commitment to teaching excellence Prepare and teach undergraduate and/or graduate classes Contribute assessment information and data as requested Mentor and/or advise undergraduate and/or graduate students Establish and/or continue an independent line of research Continue to expand professional influence in the academic discipline through research and/or publication Engage in service within the academic unit, the university, and the profession as appropriate based on teaching and research constraints Teach three (3) classes each academic year Physical Activities Working Conditions Additional Information Special Instructions Summary Important Message 1) All employees serve as a representative of the University and are expected to display respect, civility, professional courtesy, consideration of others and discretion in all interactions with members of the UT Dallas community and the general public. 2) The University of Texas at Dallas is committed to providing an educational, living, and working environment that is welcoming, respectful, and inclusive of all members of the university community. UT Dallas does not discriminate on the basis of race, color, religion, sex (including pregnancy), sexual orientation, gender identity, gender expression, age, national origin, disability, genetic information, or veteran status in its services, programs, activities, employment, and education, including in admission and enrollment. The University is committed to providing access, equal opportunity, and reasonable accommodation for individuals with disabilities. To request reasonable accommodation in the employment application and interview process, contact the ADA Coordinator . For inquiries regarding nondiscrimination policies, contact the Title IX Coordinator . Supplemental Questions Required fields are indicated with an asterisk (*). * How did you learn of this employment opportunity? Governmental Agency Job Posting University's Job Posting Agency Referral Advertisement in The Chronicle of Higher Education Advertisement in a Journal in my Academic Discipline Personal Referral Another Website Other * Which of the following most accurately describes your highest earned degree? Doctor of Philosophy (PhD) Doctor of Education (EdD) Other Doctoral Degree (DSci, DAud, MD, etc.) Specialist's Degree (EdS) Professional Degree Juris Doctor Master's Degree Bachelor's Degree Associate's Degree Certificate * In which Academic Discipline is your highest earned degree? (Open Ended Question) * How many years of relevant work experience do you have in Higher Education, Industry, or Research? None One year 2-3 years 4-5 years 6-10 years Over 10 years Applicant Documents Required Documents Cover Letter/Letter of Application Teaching Philosophy Curriculum Vitae Writing Sample Teaching Evaluations Research Statement Optional Documents Unofficial Transcripts Other Document Relevant URL Veteran Employment Preference - Form DD-214 Other Document Human Resources, 800 West Campbell Road, AD3.418 Richardson, TX 75080-3021 972.883.2221 Check Us Out on Social Media To ensure the security of your data, you will be logged out due to inactivity in 3 minutes at . Any data not saved will be lost. Click 'OK' to keep your session active."
